e('A **digital signature** lets anyone holding your public key check two things about a message: that it really came from the holder of the matching private key, and that not a single bit has changed since it was signed.','A **digital signature** lets a verifier with a trusted public key check message integrity and authenticity to the corresponding signing key, under the scheme\'s security assumptions. It does not identify a human or establish freshness by itself.','Computationalassurance not absolutehumanattribution')
e('| Proves to a third party | No | Yes |','| Verifier can check without signing power | No (shared MAC key) | Yes |','Avoidlegalproofclaim')
e('That fourth row is **non-repudiation**: because only one party could have produced the signature, a third party (a judge, an auditor, a browser) can be convinced of who signed. In practice non-repudiation is only as strong as the key custody. "My laptop was stolen" is a real defence.','Signatures can support evidence of origin because verification need not grant signing authority. Attribution also needs trustworthy identity binding, key custody and protocol context.\n\n> [!note] Content gap: legal effect\n> This lesson supplies no jurisdiction-specific legal assessment. It does not claim a signature alone proves a person acted, guarantees admissibility or determines a dispute.','Omitunsupportedlegaloutcomes',[ts])
e('If you need both, you encrypt and sign, usually by using a protocol (TLS, Signal) that combines them correctly.','If both are needed, use a suitable authenticated-encryption protocol. TLS certificate handshakes use signatures for authentication; Signal\'s message protection is not simply a publicly verifiable signature on every message.','Signalnotgenericencryptthenpublicsign',['https://signal.org/docs/specifications/doubleratchet/',tls])
e('Signature algorithms work on a fixed-size number, and they are slow. So every real scheme first hashes the message with something like SHA-256 and signs the digest.','Many signature schemes hash messages as part of their encoding, but the construction is scheme-specific. For example, pure Ed25519 hashes the message together with secret-derived material and signature/public-key data; it is not simply Sign(SHA256(message)). Follow the API\'s message or prehash interface. The diagram is only a simplified hash-then-sign construction.','Notallschemesstandaloneprehash',[ed,rsa])
e('message (any size)\n     |\n  SHA-256','simplified hash-then-sign example\nmessage\n     |\n  SHA-256','Scopeexistingdiagram',[rsa])
e('RSA signing is the RSA decryption operation applied to the hash:','The raw RSA private exponentiation resembles the RSA decryption primitive, but real signature encoding is different from encryption padding. For this arithmetic-only example,','Signatureencodingnotencryptprivate')
e('Only the holder of `d` can find 588, but anyone with `(n, e)` can check it.','Anyone can break this tiny example by factoring n or testing candidates. Real security requires large keys and a complete specified signature scheme; public verification alone is illustrated here.','Tinytoyclaimfalse')
e('**Forgery from nothing.** Pick any `s`, compute `m = s^e mod n`. Now `s` is a valid signature on `m`. The attacker cannot choose `m`, but EUF-CMA says no new valid pair should be possible at all.','**Raw-message forgery.** If the message space is the integers modulo n, pick s and compute m = s^e mod n. The pair verifies. If the scheme instead hashes an external message first, this does not by itself find a message with hash m; do not confuse those two models. Neither observation justifies inventing a signature encoding.','RawRSAforgerydoesnotautomaticallyinvertprehash')
e('deterministic, old, still everywhere (most certificate signatures).','deterministic and used in existing protocols.','Unsupportedprevalence')
e('Each signature needs a fresh secret random number `k`, the **nonce**.','Each signature needs a secret nonce k selected according to the scheme, either securely at random or with a specified deterministic derivation.','DeterministicECDSAnoncerule',[ec])
e('**Even a slightly biased `k`** leaks the key over many signatures, via lattice attacks.','**Certain nonce biases or partial leaks** can enable private-key recovery with enough signatures; exploitability depends on the bias and observations.','Notanybiasautomaticrecovery',[ec])
e('The fix most libraries now use is **deterministic nonces** (RFC 6979): derive `k` from the private key and the message hash, so it is unique per message without needing a good RNG at signing time.','One standard option is **deterministic ECDSA** (RFC 6979), deriving k from the private key and message hash. It removes reliance on fresh signing-time randomness, but is not a mathematical uniqueness guarantee across all messages. Secure randomness is still needed for key generation, and side-channel/fault defenses remain necessary.','Removeunsupporteddefaultsanduniqueness',[ec])
e('**Ed25519** (EdDSA on Curve25519, RFC 8032)','**Ed25519** (EdDSA on the Edwards-form edwards25519 curve, RFC 8032)','DistinguishX25519Montgomeryform',[ed])
e('Same message, same signature; no RNG needed.','Same key and message, same signature; no fresh per-signature RNG input is required. Key generation still needs secure randomness.','ScopeRNGrequirement',[ed])
e('The same code with ECDSA would print `False`, since each signature uses a new nonce.','ECDSA with independently random nonces would normally print False; deterministic ECDSA can instead repeat the signature for the same key and message.','ECDSAmodes',[ec])
e('| RSA-2048 | 256 B | 256 B | Slow sign, fast verify |','| RSA-2048 | 256 B modulus plus exponent/encoding | 256 B | Cost depends on exponent and implementation |','RSApublickeynotjustmodulus',[rsa])
e('| ECDSA P-256 | 64 B | 64 B | Needs a good nonce |','| ECDSA P-256 | 65 B uncompressed SEC1; 33 B compressed | 64 B fixed-width r||s | Needs a correctly generated nonce |','Publicencodingprefixmissing',['https://cryptography.io/en/latest/hazmat/primitives/asymmetric/ec/'])
e('(Sizes are raw; ECDSA signatures are usually DER-encoded, about 70–72 bytes.) For new designs Ed25519 is the default choice. ECDSA P-256 wins when you need FIPS-approved hardware or WebAuthn compatibility. RSA persists mostly for compatibility. Post-quantum signatures are covered in the last lesson of this module.','Sizes depend on encoding: DER ECDSA signatures vary, commonly around 70–72 bytes for P-256; smaller encodings can occur. Ed25519 is a useful choice when the protocol supports it. ECDSA P-256 is widely interoperable, but FIPS 186-5 also specifies EdDSA; compliance depends on a validated implementation and applicable policy. WebAuthn support depends on negotiated algorithms and authenticators. Post-quantum signatures are covered later.','EncodingandFIPSnotexclusiveP256',['https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/186-5/final','https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/'])
e('**Email**: DKIM signatures let a receiving server check a message really left the sender\'s domain.','**Email**: DKIM validates selected headers and body content against a signing domain\'s key. The signing domain need not match the visible From address; DMARC alignment is a separate check.','DKIMnotvisibleFromidentity',['https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6376'])
e('**Passkeys**: WebAuthn logs you in by signing a server challenge with a key that never leaves your device.','**Passkeys**: WebAuthn assertions sign authenticator data plus a client-data hash that binds the challenge and origin. Some passkeys synchronize between devices; device-bound and backed-up credentials have different custody models.','Passkeysdosyncandnotchallengealone',['https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/'])
e('**Compromised keys sign forever.** If a key leaks, old signatures can no longer be trusted unless they were timestamped by a third party before the leak.','**Compromised keys can create new forgeries.** Trusted timestamps or other reliable prior records can help distinguish older signatures. Whether an old artifact remains acceptable depends on the earliest plausible compromise, trust-chain validation and policy, not a universal timestamp rule.','Timestampnotsoleevidenceorautomatictrust',[ts])
